{"id":"qzpyq-chemistry-hydrocarbons-hydrocarbons-test-1","_quizrrTestId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c","title":"Hydrocarbons - Test 1","questions":[{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d928451d","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d928451d","q":"<p>Presence of which reagent will affect the reversibility of the following reaction, and change it to a irreversible reaction :</p><p>$C{H}_{4} + {I}_{2}\\mathop{&#8652;}_{Reversible}^{hv}{CH}_{3} - I + HI$</p>","options":["$HOCl$","dilute ${HNO}_{2}$","Liquid ${NH}_{3}$","Concentrated ${HIO}_{3}$"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Iodination of alkane is reversible reaction.<br />It can be irreversible in the presence of strong oxidising agent like conc. ${HNO}_{3}$ or conc. ${HIO}_{3}$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284522","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284522","q":"<p>Identify the correct statement for the below given transformation.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-a71ec7fc964f765a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 497px; height: 80px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a71ec7fc964f765a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a71ec7fc964f765a.png\"></p>","options":["<p>$A - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}CH = CH - {CH}_{3},$</p><p>$B - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}{CH}_{2}CH = {CH}_{2},$& Saytzeff products</p>","<p>$A - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}CH = CH - {CH}_{3},$</p><p>$B - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}{CH}_{2}CH = {CH}_{2},$<br />Hafmann products</p>","<p>$A - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}{CH}_{2}CH = {CH}_{2},$</p><p>$B - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}CH = {CHCH}_{3},$<br />Hofmann products</p>","<p>$A - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}{CH}_{2}CH = {CH}_{2},$</p><p>$B - {CH}_{3}{CH}_{2}CH = {CHCH}_{3},$<br />Saytzeff products</p>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The Hofmann rule states that the major alkene product is the least substituted and least stable product when it comes to asymmetrical amines.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-c45e92755365ea0d.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 451px; height: 140px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c45e92755365ea0d.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c45e92755365ea0d.png\"></p><p>So, major product will be pent-1-ene and minor product will be pent-2-ene.</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284527","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284527","q":"<p>$2 - hexene\\mathop{&#8594;}_{\\left(ii\\right){H}_{2}O}^{\\left(i\\right){O}_{3}}Products$</p><p>The two products formed in above reaction are</p>","options":["Butanal and acetaldehyde","Butanoic acid and acetaldehyde","Butanal and acetic acid","Butanoic acid and acetic acid"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Ozone will cleave carbon-carbon double bonds to give ketones/carboxylic acids after oxidative workup. Here in this reaction Cleavage occurs at unsaturation of hexene and Butanoic acid and acetic acid is formed.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-83e6bf28fca34684.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 465px; height: 84px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-83e6bf28fca34684.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-83e6bf28fca34684.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d928452c","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d928452c","q":"The number of $s{p}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals in a molecule of benzene is:","options":["$24$","$6$","$18$","$12$"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"In benzene total six ${sp}^{2}$ hybrid carbon atoms are present .Each carbon atom has $3{sp}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals. Therefore total ${sp}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals are 18 in benzene.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_via_unique_stem","_marksId":"648b5a69417cc3fb48d66deb","question":"The number of $s{p}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals in a molecule of benzene is:","explanation":"In benzene total six ${sp}^{2}$ hybrid carbon atoms are present .Each carbon atom has $3{sp}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals. Therefore total ${sp}^{2}$ hybrid orbitals are 18 in benzene."},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284531","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284531","q":"Which of the following compound will most easily be attacked by an electrophile?","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252F790eef58-bc1c-46e0-a813-5a77d46fbffc.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F790eef58-bc1c-46e0-a813-5a77d46fbffc.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F790eef58-bc1c-46e0-a813-5a77d46fbffc.png?alt=media\"><br>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252Fae6066ac-bb71-410d-84d5-784211c8b7c5.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fae6066ac-bb71-410d-84d5-784211c8b7c5.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fae6066ac-bb71-410d-84d5-784211c8b7c5.png?alt=media\"><br>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252Fb52daada-16cc-475a-a47c-9d9bc2ff9088.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb52daada-16cc-475a-a47c-9d9bc2ff9088.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb52daada-16cc-475a-a47c-9d9bc2ff9088.png?alt=media\"><br>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252Fa4a5c518-95c2-4352-a11d-068e4666a243.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fa4a5c518-95c2-4352-a11d-068e4666a243.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fa4a5c518-95c2-4352-a11d-068e4666a243.png?alt=media\"><br>"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Ortho, para directing groups are electron-donating groups; meta directing groups are electron-withdrawing groups. The halide ions, which are electron-withdrawing but ortho, para directing, are the exception.</p><p>Here $- OH$ group is strong electron donating group. Hence electrophile can easily give electron.</p><p>$- OH$ group in phenol can release electrons to the ring better than $- C{H}_{3}$ group in toluene. $Cl$ atom has electron withdrawing effect which inhibits electrophile attack readily.</p><p><br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/76de71f0-1dcd-4dba-99b5-ef165f66bbf6.png\" style=\"width: 566px; height: 112px;\"/></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login_id","_marksId":"6682bd19ba0e94a3c4025a80","question":"Which of the following compound will most easily be attacked by an electrophile?","explanation":"<p>Ortho, para directing groups are electron-donating groups; meta directing groups are electron-withdrawing groups. The halide ions, which are electron-withdrawing but ortho, para directing, are the exception.</p><p>Here $- OH$ group is strong electron donating group. Hence electrophile can easily give electron.</p><p>$- OH$ group in phenol can release electrons to the ring better than $- C{H}_{3}$ group in toluene. $Cl$ atom has electron withdrawing effect which inhibits electrophile attack readily.</p><p><br/><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/76de71f0-1dcd-4dba-99b5-ef165f66bbf6.png\" style=\"width: 566px; height: 112px;\"/></p>"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284536","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284536","q":"The alkane from below having two secondary hydrogens is :","options":["4-Ethyl-3,4-dimethyloctane","2,2,3,3-Tetramethylpentane","2,2,4,5-Tetramethylheptane","2,2,4,4-Tetramethylhexane"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d928453b","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d928453b","q":"<p>The major product of the following reaction is,<br /><br /><img height=\"60\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-eb5ea3e8b996af8e.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" width=\"244\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-eb5ea3e8b996af8e.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-eb5ea3e8b996af8e.png\"></p>","options":["$RCOOH$","$RCHO$","${RCH}_{2}{NH}_{2}$","${RCONH}_{2}$"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>In the first step, $RCN$ gets reduced to $RCH = NH$,~which then gets hydrolyzed to $RCH = O$.</p><p><img height=\"74\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-6191a65e648c398f.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" width=\"506\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6191a65e648c398f.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6191a65e648c398f.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284540","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284540","q":"<p>Major product of the following reaction is:</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-e27190ca81598057.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 164px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e27190ca81598057.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e27190ca81598057.png\"></p>","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-ab059d95ea78ddc0.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 197px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ab059d95ea78ddc0.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ab059d95ea78ddc0.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-affa70ee18fe1f9a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 191px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-affa70ee18fe1f9a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-affa70ee18fe1f9a.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-2878b5bd9b454e77.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 212px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2878b5bd9b454e77.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2878b5bd9b454e77.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-7a07559e8cf5a89b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 233px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7a07559e8cf5a89b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7a07559e8cf5a89b.png\">"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>During an electrophilic addition reactions double bonds donate lone pair electrons to an electrophile.</p><p>Markovnikov's Rule: The addition of a proton <span style=\"background-color:null;\">acid</span> to the double bond of an alkene results in a product with the acid hydrogen bound to the carbon atom that already has the greater number of hydrogen attached.</p><p>In the given reaction electrophile $D^{+}$ adds to the double bond, a carbocation is formed. Then ${Cl}^{-}$ is added at the electron deficient carbocation.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-ffb0b3bc22afa507.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 767px; height: 207px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ffb0b3bc22afa507.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ffb0b3bc22afa507.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284545","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d9284545","q":"<p>The major product formed in the following reaction is:</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-3b051ffd7c7bf4cc.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 334px; height: 114px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3b051ffd7c7bf4cc.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3b051ffd7c7bf4cc.png\"></p>","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-b38d6b4ead7fbbb1.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 282px; height: 104px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b38d6b4ead7fbbb1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b38d6b4ead7fbbb1.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0c304bab07b27824.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 104px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0c304bab07b27824.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0c304bab07b27824.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1fa09daf9b947278.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 210px; height: 95px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1fa09daf9b947278.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1fa09daf9b947278.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-04a13340f4f7b220.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 137px; height: 145px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-04a13340f4f7b220.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-04a13340f4f7b220.png\">"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-59476d642379752d.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 531px; height: 216px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-59476d642379752d.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-59476d642379752d.png\"></p><p>At $25 ° C$ thermodynamicaly controlled Product is formed as major product.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d928454a","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d928454a","q":"<p>In the given reaction sequence, the major product &#39;$C$&#39;~is :</p><p>${C}_{8}{H}_{10}\\mathop{⟶ {HNO}_{3}}_{{H}_{2}{SO}_{4}}A\\mathop{⟶ {Br}_{2}}_{&#916;}B\\mathop{⟶ alcoholic}_{KOH}C$</p>","options":["<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-f05767a4898a1192.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 98px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f05767a4898a1192.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f05767a4898a1192.png\"></p>","<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-893f93421d44dc25.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 240px; height: 77px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-893f93421d44dc25.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-893f93421d44dc25.png\"></p>","<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0494ddd30c2dd9a7.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 89px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0494ddd30c2dd9a7.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0494ddd30c2dd9a7.png\"></p>","<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-b0dcb9968c19e61a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 97px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b0dcb9968c19e61a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b0dcb9968c19e61a.png\"></p>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-e3803ba6312c9465.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 553px; height: 148px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e3803ba6312c9465.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e3803ba6312c9465.png\"></p><p>In first step nitration of benzene ring will take place at para position, since ethyl group is ortho-para directing in nature.& </p><p>Then halogenation& reaction will take place at benzyl position followed by dehydrohalogenation& reaction with alcoholic KOH.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4c"},{"id":"qz_69de4f6f2ee0e063d928454f","_quizrrId":"69de4f6f2ee0e063d928454f","q":"<p>The major product of the following reaction is:<br /><br /><img height=\"123\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-c8f717ce56a0e8c5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" width=\"419\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c8f717ce56a0e8c5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c8f